  'text',
  $q5314${"topic":"health_sports","wordCount":154,"questionCount":5}$q5314$::jsonb,
  $q74410${"mode":"reading","level":"B2","format":"text","topic":"Health & Sports","title":"Turning at the Second Bridge","story":"After a month away from running, Yusuf wanted his old route along the canal, which took about forty minutes at an easy pace. His physiotherapist had cleared him to jog, but only if he stopped at the first sharp pain and kept the distance short. He told his friend Farid they would turn at the second bridge, not at the lock where they used to finish. Farid agreed to match that slower plan instead of going ahead. They set off in the cool evening, and Yusuf noticed his calf felt tight but not painful. At the bridge he was tempted to continue, because his breathing felt easy. He remembered the instruction and turned around. The walk back counted as part of the session. At home he wrote the time and the fact that the tight feeling faded after he stretched. He would add a little more next week only if the calf stayed calm.","dialogue":[],"vocabulary":[{"word":"pace","definition":"The speed of a run or a walk."},{"word":"physiotherapist","definition":"A specialist who treats joints and muscles with exercise."},{"word":"calf","definition":"The fleshy back of the lower leg."},{"word":"tempted","definition":"Strongly drawn towards a choice that may be unwise."},{"word":"stretched","definition":"Gently lengthened a muscle after exercise."},{"word":"session","definition":"One period of training."}],"quiz":[{"question":"Where did the two friends agree to turn?","options":["At the second bridge, not at their old finish","At the lock, as they had always done","At home, before they started running","Wherever Yusuf's breathing felt easiest"],"correctAnswer":"At the second bridge, not at their old finish","explanation":"Yusuf said they would turn at the second bridge, not at the lock where they used to finish.","supportingSentence":"He told his friend Farid they would turn at the second bridge, not at the lock where they used to finish.","skill":"detail"},{"question":"What conditions did the physiotherapist set?","options":["He had to repeat the full old route that evening","He was told not to stretch after the run","He could jog, but had to stop for sharp pain and stay short","He could run only if Farid went ahead"],"correctAnswer":"He could jog, but had to stop for sharp pain and stay short","explanation":"He was cleared to jog only if he stopped at the first sharp pain and kept the distance short.","supportingSentence":"His physiotherapist had cleared him to jog, but only if he stopped at the first sharp pain and kept the distance short.","skill":"detail"},{"question":"What nearly made Yusuf pass the agreed turning point?","options":["Farid refused to turn with him","The specialist was waiting further along the canal","His breathing felt easy, so continuing seemed possible","A sharp pain forced him to sprint"],"correctAnswer":"His breathing felt easy, so continuing seemed possible","explanation":"He was tempted to continue because his breathing felt easy.","supportingSentence":"At the bridge he was tempted to continue, because his breathing felt easy.","skill":"inference"},{"question":"How should the walk back be understood?","options":["As a race against Farid to the lock","As a reason to skip the notes at home","As proof that the evening had been wasted","As part of the training rather than a failed run"],"correctAnswer":"As part of the training rather than a failed run","explanation":"The walk back counted as part of the session, so turning early was still training.","supportingSentence":"The walk back counted as part of the session.","skill":"inference"},{"question":"What does tempted mean in this story?","options":["Drawn towards an unwise choice","Ordered by a specialist to stop","Told to write the time at home","Matched to a slower friend's plan"],"correctAnswer":"Drawn towards an unwise choice","explanation":"He was tempted to continue past the plan, so he was drawn towards a choice he should not make.","supportingSentence":"At the bridge he was tempted to continue, because his breathing felt easy.","skill":"vocabulary"}],"wordCount":154,"questionCount":5}$q74410$::jsonb,
  '47d2fbeb33b71b314ae84529e152f34932e9c7f8e509771a14f8d94029e33ce4',
  true
) on conflict (content_hash) do nothing;

insert into public.exercise_bank (
  id, kind, level, exercise_type, dimensions, payload, content_hash, active
) values (
  '3bc326e1-58e7-428f-a3ec-2eb7e9689330'::uuid,
  'reading',
  'B2',
  'text',
  $q5312${"topic":"health_sports","wordCount":152,"questionCount":5}$q5312$::jsonb,
  $q99915${"mode":"reading","level":"B2","format":"text","topic":"Health & Sports","title":"Four Lengths and a Pause","story":"Greta had swum for years, yet a sore shoulder made her usual crawl stroke impossible. The pool coach watched two lengths and asked her to stop before she began to compensate with her neck. He suggested breaststroke for a fortnight, with a kickboard on days when even that pull hurt. Greta felt embarrassed, because the slow lane was full of beginners and she had once trained with the club squad. The coach said fitness could be protected in more than one way, and that pride was a poor reason to delay healing. By the second week her shoulder ached less in the morning, and she could dress without a careful shrug. She still avoided diving. At the end of the fortnight the coach let her swim four lengths of crawl, then return to the gentler stroke if the ache came back. Greta learned that pausing a habit was different from losing it.","dialogue":[],"vocabulary":[{"word":"compensate","definition":"To make up for a weak part by using another."},{"word":"breaststroke","definition":"A swimming stroke with both arms moving together."},{"word":"fortnight","definition":"A period of two weeks."},{"word":"embarrassed","definition":"Feeling awkward or ashamed."},{"word":"diving","definition":"Going into water head first."},{"word":"ache","definition":"A dull pain that lasts."}],"quiz":[{"question":"What swimming plan did the coach give Greta?","options":["Crawl only, so the sore shoulder would toughen","Breaststroke for two weeks, and a kickboard if the pull hurt","No swimming until she felt no awkwardness","Diving practice with the club squad"],"correctAnswer":"Breaststroke for two weeks, and a kickboard if the pull hurt","explanation":"He suggested breaststroke for a fortnight, and a kickboard on days when the pull hurt.","supportingSentence":"He suggested breaststroke for a fortnight, with a kickboard on days when even that pull hurt.","skill":"detail"},{"question":"What was Greta allowed to try at the end of that period?","options":["Four lengths of crawl, then the gentler stroke if pain returned","Two lengths using her neck to help the arm","A full squad session with dives","Nothing further, even if the morning felt better"],"correctAnswer":"Four lengths of crawl, then the gentler stroke if pain returned","explanation":"The coach let her swim four lengths of crawl, then return to the gentler stroke if the ache came back.","supportingSentence":"At the end of the fortnight the coach let her swim four lengths of crawl, then return to the gentler stroke if the ache came back.","skill":"detail"},{"question":"Why did the slow lane embarrass Greta?","options":["The coach told her she would never swim again","The kickboard was forbidden in that lane","Her neck pain had started among the beginners","She was swimming among beginners after once training with the squad"],"correctAnswer":"She was swimming among beginners after once training with the squad","explanation":"She felt embarrassed because the slow lane was full of beginners and she had trained with the squad.","supportingSentence":"Greta felt embarrassed, because the slow lane was full of beginners and she had once trained with the club squad.","skill":"inference"},{"question":"What idea did Greta take from the two weeks?","options":["Stopping a habit for a while is not the same as losing it","Four lengths of crawl replace every other stroke","Pride is a good reason to swim through pain","A sore shoulder means a swimmer should quit"],"correctAnswer":"Stopping a habit for a while is not the same as losing it","explanation":"She learned that pausing a habit was different from losing it.","supportingSentence":"Greta learned that pausing a habit was different from losing it.","skill":"inference"},{"question":"What does compensate mean here?","options":["Swim faster than the club squad","Let another part of the body take over the work","Feel awkward in front of beginners","Rest for a period of two weeks"],"correctAnswer":"Let another part of the body take over the work","explanation":"The coach stopped her before she could compensate with her neck, meaning use her neck to do the shoulder's work.","supportingSentence":"The pool coach watched two lengths and asked her to stop before she began to compensate with her neck.","skill":"vocabulary"}],"wordCount":152,"questionCount":5}$q99915$::jsonb,
  '3bc326e158e7328f13ec2eb7e9689330d436db3557065a3ae18a35d9c32ae498',
  true
) on conflict (content_hash) do nothing;

insert into public.exercise_bank (
  id, kind, level, exercise_type, dimensions, payload, content_hash, active
) values (
  'd6291587-b585-48dc-af61-a94226f40192'::uuid,
  'reading',
  'B2',
  'text',
  $q5196${"topic":"culture_food","wordCount":153,"questionCount":5}$q5196$::jsonb,
  $q71999${"mode":"reading","level":"B2","format":"text","topic":"Culture & Food","title":"Three Trays, One Oven","story":"For the street festival, three families agreed to bake in one kitchen, because the hall oven was already booked. Aunt Rosa brought savoury pastries from her coastal hometown, and the Nguyen family brought a rice cake that needed a low, gentle heat. The pastries wanted a short burst of a hotter oven. Mina wrote the order on a strip of paper and taped it to the cupboard. The cake would go first, then the pastries, then jam buns a neighbour had dropped off. They set a phone alarm for each change so nobody would trust memory alone. When the hall opened, every dish was labelled with its name and its main ingredients. A child asked why the rice cake was only faintly sweet. Mina said it was meant to follow the salty pastries, not to compete with the buns. People took a little of each, which was the sharing the families had wanted.","dialogue":[],"vocabulary":[{"word":"festival","definition":"A public celebration with shared events."},{"word":"savoury","definition":"Tasting salty or spiced rather than sweet."},{"word":"ingredients","definition":"The foods that are used to make a dish."},{"word":"faintly","definition":"Only slightly, not strongly."},{"word":"compete","definition":"To try to stand out more than something else."},{"word":"labelled","definition":"Marked with a name or a short description."}],"quiz":[{"question":"In what order were the foods baked?","options":["The cake first, then the pastries, then the jam buns","Only the pastries, for the whole afternoon","The cake and the pastries at the same moment","The jam buns first and the cake not at all"],"correctAnswer":"The cake first, then the pastries, then the jam buns","explanation":"The cake would go first, then the pastries, then the jam buns.","supportingSentence":"The cake would go first, then the pastries, then jam buns a neighbour had dropped off.","skill":"detail"},{"question":"Why did they set an alarm for each change of food?","options":["The hall oven had to be booked again","The pastries needed to stay hot all day","A child had asked them to hide the cake","They did not want to rely on memory alone"],"correctAnswer":"They did not want to rely on memory alone","explanation":"They set a phone alarm for each change so nobody would trust memory alone.","supportingSentence":"They set a phone alarm for each change so nobody would trust memory alone.","skill":"detail"},{"question":"Why was the rice cake only slightly sweet?","options":["Mina had left it in a cold oven","The families had no sweet ingredients left","It was meant to follow salty food, not to outdo the buns","A child had asked for it to taste like jam"],"correctAnswer":"It was meant to follow salty food, not to outdo the buns","explanation":"Mina said the cake was meant to follow the salty pastries and not compete with the buns.","supportingSentence":"Mina said it was meant to follow the salty pastries, not to compete with the buns.","skill":"inference"},{"question":"How can you tell the families got the sharing they wanted?","options":["The name cards were taken off the table","People took a little food from each dish","One child ate only the jam buns","The hall oven booking was cancelled"],"correctAnswer":"People took a little food from each dish","explanation":"People took a little of each, which was the sharing the families had wanted.","supportingSentence":"People took a little of each, which was the sharing the families had wanted.","skill":"inference"},{"question":"What does savoury mean in this story?","options":["Salty or spiced rather than sweet","Written on a paper label","Shared among three families","Baked for a very long time"],"correctAnswer":"Salty or spiced rather than sweet","explanation":"The savoury pastries are the salty ones the cake was meant to follow, so they are not a sweet food.","supportingSentence":"Aunt Rosa brought savoury pastries from her coastal hometown, and the Nguyen family brought a rice cake that needed a low, gentle heat.","skill":"vocabulary"}],"wordCount":153,"questionCount":5}$q71999$::jsonb,
  'd6291587b585f8dcdf61a94226f40192c84cad5f961cda7b6918f541b0f4110d',
  true
) on conflict (content_hash) do nothing;

insert into public.exercise_bank (
  id, kind, level, exercise_type, dimensions, payload, content_hash, active
) values (
  '2c7bf6e1-07ff-485d-a6fe-adf869dc5272'::uuid,
  'reading',
  'B2',
  'text',
  $q5194${"topic":"culture_food","wordCount":151,"questionCount":5}$q5194$::jsonb,
  $q88645${"mode":"reading","level":"B2","format":"text","topic":"Culture & Food","title":"Chilli in a Side Bowl","story":"The junior club ended its season with a potluck supper in the sports hall. Each household was asked to bring one dish and a card listing what was in it, because two players had food allergies. Tomas arrived with a bean stew his grandmother had taught him, mild enough for children, with pickled chilli served in a separate bowl. He wrote that choice clearly on the card so nobody would be surprised by the heat. During the meal a parent thanked him, because her son could eat the stew without avoiding the whole pot. The coach then asked him to help write a guide so strong flavour could be offered beside a dish instead of being stirred through it. Tomas agreed. He went home with an empty pot and a recipe for oat biscuits from another family. The supper was simple, but people could eat together without guessing what was safe.","dialogue":[],"vocabulary":[{"word":"potluck","definition":"A shared meal where each person brings some food."},{"word":"allergies","definition":"Conditions in which particular foods make someone ill."},{"word":"pickled","definition":"Preserved in vinegar or brine for a sharp taste."},{"word":"flavour","definition":"The taste of a food."},{"word":"stirred","definition":"Mixed through a dish."},{"word":"biscuits","definition":"Small baked cakes, often sweet and crisp."}],"quiz":[{"question":"Why did each household have to list the contents of its dish?","options":["The hall had banned bean stew","Tomas could not read a recipe","Two players had foods that could make them ill","The coach allowed only sweet biscuits"],"correctAnswer":"Two players had foods that could make them ill","explanation":"Each household brought a card listing the contents because two players had food allergies.","supportingSentence":"Each household was asked to bring one dish and a card listing what was in it, because two players had food allergies.","skill":"detail"},{"question":"How was the sharp topping kept apart from the stew?","options":["It was spread on the oat biscuits","It was left off the information card","It was mixed through every serving","It was served in its own bowl"],"correctAnswer":"It was served in its own bowl","explanation":"The pickled chilli was served in a separate bowl, while the stew itself was mild.","supportingSentence":"Tomas arrived with a bean stew his grandmother had taught him, mild enough for children, with pickled chilli served in a separate bowl.","skill":"detail"},{"question":"Why did one parent thank Tomas?","options":["Her son could eat the stew without having to skip the pot","The coach had cancelled the supper","She wanted the sharp topping mixed into every bowl","Tomas had forgotten the information card"],"correctAnswer":"Her son could eat the stew without having to skip the pot","explanation":"She thanked him because her son could eat the stew without avoiding the whole pot.","supportingSentence":"During the meal a parent thanked him, because her son could eat the stew without avoiding the whole pot.","skill":"inference"},{"question":"What was the result of the careful food information?","options":["The club stopped serving food the next year","People could eat together without guessing what was safe","Guests left before any food was opened","Every dish had to be the same stew"],"correctAnswer":"People could eat together without guessing what was safe","explanation":"The supper let people eat together without guessing what was safe.","supportingSentence":"The supper was simple, but people could eat together without guessing what was safe.","skill":"inference"},{"question":"What does allergies mean in this story?","options":["A mild stew taught by a relative","Sweet oat cakes from another family","Harmful reactions that some foods can cause","Cards placed beside a supper dish"],"correctAnswer":"Harmful reactions that some foods can cause","explanation":"The cards were required because two players had food allergies, meaning some foods could make them ill.","supportingSentence":"Each household was asked to bring one dish and a card listing what was in it, because two players had food allergies.","skill":"vocabulary"}],"wordCount":151,"questionCount":5}$q88645$::jsonb,
  '2c7bf6e107ffa85d06feadf869dc527252900f4a649dc885e3e6771d3df180dd',
  true
) on conflict (content_hash) do nothing;

insert into public.exercise_bank (
  id, kind, level, exercise_type, dimensions, payload, content_hash, active
) values (
  '26018bb0-39cc-4ef0-a928-5691c3c48edc'::uuid,
  'reading',
  'B2',
  'text',
  $q4988${"topic":"technology","wordCount":152,"questionCount":5}$q4988$::jsonb,
  $q86698${"mode":"reading","level":"B2","format":"text","topic":"Technology","title":"A Second Home for Photos","story":"The community centre offered a free class on keeping photos safe, and the chairs filled with people who had never made a backup. Nora asked everyone to bring a phone and, if possible, a charger. Halfway through the demonstration, the plug board failed, and three phones were close to switching off. She did not cancel the hour. She borrowed a power bank from the desk and let those learners take turns, while the others drew the steps on paper. The steps were to choose an album, copy it, and check that the picture still opened. When the plug board was replaced, the waiting phones had enough charge to finish. One man found that his only pictures of a wedding lived on a cracked screen. He completed that copy before any optional extra. Nora said a backup was a second home for a picture, and it was useless if it was never made.","dialogue":[],"vocabulary":[{"word":"backup","definition":"A second copy kept in case the first is lost."},{"word":"charger","definition":"A device that puts power back into a phone."},{"word":"demonstration","definition":"A live showing of how to do something."},{"word":"album","definition":"A set of photographs kept together."},{"word":"cracked","definition":"Split or broken across the surface."},{"word":"optional","definition":"Possible to do, but not required."}],"quiz":[{"question":"What failed during the photo class?","options":["The paper notes, which nobody could read","The wedding album, which was deleted on purpose","The reception desk, which closed the building","The plug board, when three phones were nearly out of power"],"correctAnswer":"The plug board, when three phones were nearly out of power","explanation":"Halfway through the demonstration, the plug board failed and three phones were close to switching off.","supportingSentence":"Halfway through the demonstration, the plug board failed, and three phones were close to switching off.","skill":"detail"},{"question":"Which actions made up the copying steps?","options":["Choose a set of photos, copy it, and check that a picture opens","Cancel the hour and throw the paper away","Crack the screen and then skip the copy","Buy a phone, a charger, and a power bank"],"correctAnswer":"Choose a set of photos, copy it, and check that a picture opens","explanation":"The steps were to choose an album, copy it, and check that the picture still opened.","supportingSentence":"The steps were to choose an album, copy it, and check that the picture still opened.","skill":"detail"},{"question":"Why was one learner's copy more urgent than the extras?","options":["His phone was the only one still fully charged","His only wedding pictures were on a damaged screen","He had already stored the pictures in three places","Nora told him to skip the copy"],"correctAnswer":"His only wedding pictures were on a damaged screen","explanation":"His only pictures of a wedding lived on a cracked screen, so losing that screen would lose the pictures.","supportingSentence":"One man found that his only pictures of a wedding lived on a cracked screen.","skill":"inference"},{"question":"How did Nora deal with the power problem?","options":["She kept the class going with a shared battery and paper notes","She refused to let anyone draw the steps","She sent the three learners home at once","She deleted the albums to save power"],"correctAnswer":"She kept the class going with a shared battery and paper notes","explanation":"She borrowed a power bank and let others draw the steps, so the class continued without the plug board.","supportingSentence":"She borrowed a power bank from the desk and let those learners take turns, while the others drew the steps on paper.","skill":"inference"},{"question":"What does optional mean in this story?","options":["Broken across the screen","The only copy of a picture","A second place where a photo lives","Not required, so it can wait"],"correctAnswer":"Not required, so it can wait","explanation":"He finished the important copy before any optional extra, so the extra was not required.","supportingSentence":"He completed that copy before any optional extra.","skill":"vocabulary"}],"wordCount":152,"questionCount":5}$q86698$::jsonb,
  '26018bb039cc7ef009285691c3c48edc7bd39335968a8de577412a12ce1d94e2',
  true
) on conflict (content_hash) do nothing;

insert into public.exercise_bank (
  id, kind, level, exercise_type, dimensions, payload, content_hash, active
) values (
  '90262818-dc6e-4b31-aaf0-2aa7b1c02c9d'::uuid,
  'reading',
  'B2',
  'text',
  $q4994${"topic":"technology","wordCount":149,"questionCount":5}$q4994$::jsonb,
  $q85952${"mode":"reading","level":"B2","format":"text","topic":"Technology","title":"One Job for Each Drawer","story":"The repair bench at the youth club had become a jumble of cables, screws, and unnamed chargers. Jamal offered to sort it before the Saturday clinic, when neighbours brought radios and lamps. He grouped items that did the same job, then printed plain labels, because handwriting had smeared there before. Each drawer received one job, from power leads to fuses and tools that had to stay in the room. He tied a tag to every charger naming the device it fit. During the clinic a helper found a fuse in seconds instead of tipping the box onto the bench. A visitor started to pocket a spare lead, and the words on the drawer gave Jamal a polite reason to stop her. Afterwards he photographed the drawers so a later volunteer could restore the same order. The system was plain, yet the hour went to repairs rather than to searching.","dialogue":[],"vocabulary":[{"word":"jumble","definition":"An untidy mixture of things."},{"word":"clinic","definition":"A session where people bring items to be repaired."},{"word":"fuses","definition":"Small parts that protect an electrical circuit."},{"word":"photographed","definition":"Made a picture of something with a camera."},{"word":"volunteer","definition":"A person who helps without being paid."},{"word":"chargers","definition":"Devices that supply power to equipment."}],"quiz":[{"question":"How were the drawers organised?","options":["Every drawer held a mix of unnamed items","Handwritten notes replaced every label","Each drawer was given a single kind of job","Radios and lamps filled the fuse drawer"],"correctAnswer":"Each drawer was given a single kind of job","explanation":"Each drawer received one job, from power leads to fuses and tools that had to stay in the room.","supportingSentence":"Each drawer received one job, from power leads to fuses and tools that had to stay in the room.","skill":"detail"},{"question":"How did Jamal stop a visitor taking a spare lead?","options":["The written label gave him a polite reason to refuse","He tipped the whole box onto the bench","He closed the clinic before she arrived","He let her keep it because the tag was blank"],"correctAnswer":"The written label gave him a polite reason to refuse","explanation":"The words on the drawer gave him a polite reason to stop her pocketing a spare lead.","supportingSentence":"A visitor started to pocket a spare lead, and the words on the drawer gave Jamal a polite reason to stop her.","skill":"detail"},{"question":"What difference did the sorting make during the repair session?","options":["Fuses became harder to find than before","Tools were repaired by spilling them on the floor","Helpers had to search every unnamed charger","A small part could be found at once without emptying the box"],"correctAnswer":"A small part could be found at once without emptying the box","explanation":"A helper found a fuse in seconds instead of tipping the box out, so the sort made parts easy to find.","supportingSentence":"During the clinic a helper found a fuse in seconds instead of tipping the box onto the bench.","skill":"inference"},{"question":"What was the practical result of the simple system?","options":["The session was spent fixing things rather than hunting for them","Handwriting replaced the printed labels","The bench was mixed up again before the hour ended","Neighbours stopped bringing radios and lamps"],"correctAnswer":"The session was spent fixing things rather than hunting for them","explanation":"The hour went to repairs rather than to searching.","supportingSentence":"The system was plain, yet the hour went to repairs rather than to searching.","skill":"inference"},{"question":"What does jumble mean in this story?","options":["A printed label on a drawer","A picture used to restore order","An untidy mix of objects","A Saturday repair for neighbours"],"correctAnswer":"An untidy mix of objects","explanation":"The bench had become a jumble of cables, screws, and chargers, meaning an untidy mix.","supportingSentence":"The repair bench at the youth club had become a jumble of cables, screws, and unnamed chargers.","skill":"vocabulary"}],"wordCount":149,"questionCount":5}$q85952$::jsonb,
  '90262818dc6e9b316af02aa7b1c02c9d80cc2fc40cc727efa0491d672bbd80e6',
  true
) on conflict (content_hash) do nothing;

insert into public.exercise_bank (
  id, kind, level, exercise_type, dimensions, payload, content_hash, active
) values (
  '1fafd73f-d327-4a4d-a4c4-94807a0235b0'::uuid,
  'reading',
  'B2',
  'text',
  $q4634${"topic":"science","wordCount":152,"questionCount":5}$q4634$::jsonb,
  $q82426${"mode":"reading","level":"B2","format":"text","topic":"Science","title":"Taller Is Not the Same","story":"The class tested whether identical seedlings grew better in full sun or in light shade. They planted two trays of the same lettuce in the same compost and gave each tray the same water every morning. One tray sat on the open bench. The other sat under a cloth that let light through but softened the midday heat. Every Monday they measured the height and wrote it in a shared table. After three weeks the shaded plants were taller, but their stems were thin and they leaned. The sunny plants were shorter and sturdier, with deeper colour in the leaves. Amina said that taller did not always mean healthier. The class then counted how many plants stayed upright after a windy night, and more of the sunny ones did. Their conclusion stayed careful. Shade had changed the shape of the growth, and one measurement would have told only part of the story.","dialogue":[],"vocabulary":[{"word":"seedlings","definition":"Very young plants grown from seed."},{"word":"compost","definition":"A rich soil mix used for growing plants."},{"word":"sturdier","definition":"Stronger and less likely to bend or break."},{"word":"upright","definition":"Standing straight, not leaning."},{"word":"measurement","definition":"A result found by measuring."},{"word":"shade","definition":"An area protected from full sun."}],"quiz":[{"question":"What did the class keep the same for both trays?","options":["The windy night and the leaf colour","The height of every stem after three weeks","The lettuce, the soil mix, and the water each morning","The cloth, the shade, and the midday heat"],"correctAnswer":"The lettuce, the soil mix, and the water each morning","explanation":"Both trays had the same lettuce, the same compost, and the same water every morning.","supportingSentence":"They planted two trays of the same lettuce in the same compost and gave each tray the same water every morning.","skill":"detail"},{"question":"How did the shaded plants look after three weeks?","options":["They had the same height and colour as day one","They had all fallen before any measuring","They were shorter and harder to bend","They were taller, but thin and leaning"],"correctAnswer":"They were taller, but thin and leaning","explanation":"After three weeks the shaded plants were taller, but their stems were thin and they leaned.","supportingSentence":"After three weeks the shaded plants were taller, but their stems were thin and they leaned.","skill":"detail"},{"question":"What point was Amina making about the tall plants?","options":["Extra height did not by itself prove they were healthier","The sunny plants had failed because they were short","Height should be the only number in the table","Lettuce grows well only under a cloth"],"correctAnswer":"Extra height did not by itself prove they were healthier","explanation":"Amina said that taller did not always mean healthier.","supportingSentence":"Amina said that taller did not always mean healthier.","skill":"inference"},{"question":"Why would one height check have been a weak conclusion?","options":["It would have given one tray more water than the other","It would have made both trays the same colour","It would have shown only part of how the plants changed","It would have proved that shade has no effect"],"correctAnswer":"It would have shown only part of how the plants changed","explanation":"Shade changed the shape of the growth, and one measurement would have told only part of the story.","supportingSentence":"Shade had changed the shape of the growth, and one measurement would have told only part of the story.","skill":"inference"},{"question":"What does sturdier mean in this story?","options":["Stronger and less likely to bend","Hidden under a garden cloth","Written as a number in a table","Taller, with a thin stem"],"correctAnswer":"Stronger and less likely to bend","explanation":"The sunny plants were shorter and sturdier, unlike the thin leaning ones, so they were stronger.","supportingSentence":"The sunny plants were shorter and sturdier, with deeper colour in the leaves.","skill":"vocabulary"}],"wordCount":152,"questionCount":5}$q82426$::jsonb,
  '1fafd73fd327da4d74c494807a0235b0a5e0d05d35b8d4535a44ad6160d13fc4',
  true
) on conflict (content_hash) do nothing;

insert into public.exercise_bank (
  id, kind, level, exercise_type, dimensions, payload, content_hash, active
) values (
  'ee2515dd-0ce6-4bf1-af72-638100fd2afa'::uuid,
