  'text',
  $q4638${"topic":"science","wordCount":147,"questionCount":5}$q4638$::jsonb,
  $q77602${"mode":"reading","level":"B2","format":"text","topic":"Science","title":"Thermometers on the Steps","story":"The science club asked why the library steps stayed icy after the playground had thawed. They guessed that the steps, set against a north wall, simply got less sun. To test that, they placed two cheap thermometers, one on the third step and one on the open path, and read them at the same hours for five days. They also noted cloud, wind, and whether salt had been spread. The step was colder each morning. By noon the path was often above freezing while the step was not. Hana saw that a handrail shadow covered the step until late, which the wall by itself did not explain. They added a sketch of that shadow to the report. The librarian used the chart to decide where to put a mat and when to spread salt. A written record had answered a question people usually met with a shrug.","dialogue":[],"vocabulary":[{"word":"thawed","definition":"Warmed enough for ice to melt."},{"word":"thermometers","definition":"Tools that measure temperature."},{"word":"freezing","definition":"Cold enough for water to turn to ice."},{"word":"shadow","definition":"A dark patch where light is blocked."},{"word":"chart","definition":"A written record of results."},{"word":"shrug","definition":"A movement of the shoulders showing you have no answer."}],"quiz":[{"question":"How did the club collect the temperature readings?","options":["They measured only after the playground melted","They left both tools indoors all week","They read two thermometers at the same hours for five days","They checked one step once, at noon"],"correctAnswer":"They read two thermometers at the same hours for five days","explanation":"They placed two thermometers and read them at the same hours for five days.","supportingSentence":"To test that, they placed two cheap thermometers, one on the third step and one on the open path, and read them at the same hours for five days.","skill":"detail"},{"question":"What was often true of the two places by noon?","options":["The step was warmer because of the salt","The tools had been taken inside","Both places were equally warm at sunrise","The path was warmer than freezing and the step was not"],"correctAnswer":"The path was warmer than freezing and the step was not","explanation":"By noon the path was often above freezing while the step was not.","supportingSentence":"By noon the path was often above freezing while the step was not.","skill":"detail"},{"question":"What extra cause did Hana notice for the cold step?","options":["A handrail blocked the sun from the step until late","Salt on the path was creating the ice","The north wall was giving the step extra sunshine","The playground melted because of the tools"],"correctAnswer":"A handrail blocked the sun from the step until late","explanation":"A handrail shadow covered the step until late, which the wall by itself did not explain.","supportingSentence":"Hana saw that a handrail shadow covered the step until late, which the wall by itself did not explain.","skill":"inference"},{"question":"What does the ending say about the club's written results?","options":["The club decided the question had no answer","The notes answered something people usually dismissed","The librarian refused to look at the chart","A shrug was more accurate than the readings"],"correctAnswer":"The notes answered something people usually dismissed","explanation":"A written record answered a question that people usually met with a shrug.","supportingSentence":"A written record had answered a question people usually met with a shrug.","skill":"inference"},{"question":"What does thawed mean in this story?","options":["Covered by a rail's dark patch","Left salty until the next noon","Warmed so that ice had melted","Measured with two tools"],"correctAnswer":"Warmed so that ice had melted","explanation":"The playground had thawed while the steps stayed icy, so the ice in the playground had melted.","supportingSentence":"The science club asked why the library steps stayed icy after the playground had thawed.","skill":"vocabulary"}],"wordCount":147,"questionCount":5}$q77602$::jsonb,
  'ee2515dd0ce61bf17f72638100fd2afae3064f6cd06a569938a47503121088e3',
  true
) on conflict (content_hash) do nothing;

insert into public.exercise_bank (
  id, kind, level, exercise_type, dimensions, payload, content_hash, active
) values (
  '8db0d721-022e-439e-a18c-7c377498860c'::uuid,
  'reading',
  'B2',
  'text',
  $q5486${"topic":"economy_finance","wordCount":153,"questionCount":5}$q5486$::jsonb,
  $q78828${"mode":"reading","level":"B2","format":"text","topic":"Economy / Finance","title":"Counting the Tin at Noon","story":"Before the craft market, Leila counted the cash float for her stall and wrote the sum in a notebook. She kept coins for change in a tin and larger notes in a pouch, so a busy moment would not mix them. Her first customer paid with a large note, and she nearly gave too much change while talking. After that she used a written price list and a calculator, and she checked the result before she handed the coins over. At lunch she counted the tin. It matched the sales she had ticked, so the morning had been accurate. A friend said she could ignore the count until evening. Leila refused, because a shortage found late was harder to explain than one found at noon. When the market closed, the pouch, the tin, and the ticks agreed. She deposited the takings on Monday and left the same float ready for the next stall.","dialogue":[],"vocabulary":[{"word":"float","definition":"A sum of cash kept so a seller can give change."},{"word":"pouch","definition":"A small soft case that closes."},{"word":"calculator","definition":"A device used for working out sums."},{"word":"shortage","definition":"An amount that is less than it should be."},{"word":"deposited","definition":"Put money into a bank or another safe place."},{"word":"takings","definition":"Money received from sales."}],"quiz":[{"question":"How did Leila separate the stall's cash?","options":["Change coins went in a tin and larger notes in a pouch","All the cash stayed in one coat pocket","The starting sum stayed at home","Prices were kept in the tin with the coins"],"correctAnswer":"Change coins went in a tin and larger notes in a pouch","explanation":"She kept coins for change in a tin and larger notes in a pouch.","supportingSentence":"She kept coins for change in a tin and larger notes in a pouch, so a busy moment would not mix them.","skill":"detail"},{"question":"Why did Leila refuse to leave the count until evening?","options":["Her friend had already taken the sales to the bank","The first customer had taken the notebook","A gap found late would be harder to explain","The calculator worked only at night"],"correctAnswer":"A gap found late would be harder to explain","explanation":"She refused because a shortage found late was harder to explain than one found at noon.","supportingSentence":"Leila refused, because a shortage found late was harder to explain than one found at noon.","skill":"detail"},{"question":"What risk showed up with the first customer?","options":["Chatting while she made change nearly caused a mistake","The pouch was stolen during the conversation","She charged twice the listed price","The customer refused to pay with a note"],"correctAnswer":"Chatting while she made change nearly caused a mistake","explanation":"She nearly gave too much change while talking, so doing the sum during a conversation was risky.","supportingSentence":"Her first customer paid with a large note, and she nearly gave too much change while talking.","skill":"inference"},{"question":"What did the midday count tell Leila about the morning?","options":["The tin was short and the marks were useless","The starting sum had already gone to the bank","She should ignore cash until the market closed","The money agreed with the sales she had marked"],"correctAnswer":"The money agreed with the sales she had marked","explanation":"The tin matched the sales she had ticked, so the morning count was accurate.","supportingSentence":"It matched the sales she had ticked, so the morning had been accurate.","skill":"inference"},{"question":"What does shortage mean in this story?","options":["Less money than the records say there should be","A written list of prices","Coins kept ready for change","A long talk with a customer"],"correctAnswer":"Less money than the records say there should be","explanation":"A shortage in the tin would mean the cash was less than the sales record, and a late one would be hard to explain.","supportingSentence":"Leila refused, because a shortage found late was harder to explain than one found at noon.","skill":"vocabulary"}],"wordCount":153,"questionCount":5}$q78828$::jsonb,
  '8db0d721022ea39e618c7c377498860c209414114645823b84c0a98468bd86e7',
  true
) on conflict (content_hash) do nothing;

insert into public.exercise_bank (
  id, kind, level, exercise_type, dimensions, payload, content_hash, active
) values (
  '235fe2cd-8668-4d8e-a803-69ccd2c9cce5'::uuid,
  'reading',
  'B2',
  'text',
  $q5485${"topic":"economy_finance","wordCount":152,"questionCount":5}$q5485$::jsonb,
  $q75905${"mode":"reading","level":"B2","format":"text","topic":"Economy / Finance","title":"The Loaf and the Roll","story":"The corner bakery had kept the same price for a plain loaf for two years. In March, Samir pinned a notice on the board saying that flour and energy cost more, so the loaf would cost a little more from Monday. A few regulars grumbled, and one said she might buy bread less often. Samir did not argue in the queue. The notice also promised that the smaller roll would stay the same price, so a tight budget still had an option. During the week he watched what people chose. Most still bought the loaf, and a few switched to the roll. On Friday the customer who had grumbled said the bread was worth the new price and bought her usual two loaves. Samir left the notice up for a month so the change could not be called a secret. He wrote both prices in his accounts in case costs fell later.","dialogue":[],"vocabulary":[{"word":"notice","definition":"A short public message put up to be read."},{"word":"energy","definition":"Power, such as electricity or gas, used to run something."},{"word":"grumbled","definition":"Complained in a low, unhappy way."},{"word":"budget","definition":"The amount of money a person can afford to spend."},{"word":"switched","definition":"Changed from one choice to another."},{"word":"accounts","definition":"Written records of money coming in and going out."}],"quiz":[{"question":"What reason did the bakery give for the new loaf price?","options":["The shop was closing on Monday","Customers had asked for a smaller roll only","Flour and the cost of running the ovens had risen","The old price had been kept secret for a month"],"correctAnswer":"Flour and the cost of running the ovens had risen","explanation":"The notice said flour and energy cost more, so the loaf would cost a little more.","supportingSentence":"In March, Samir pinned a notice on the board saying that flour and energy cost more, so the loaf would cost a little more from Monday.","skill":"detail"},{"question":"Which price stayed the same?","options":["The price written in last year's book","The price of the smaller roll","The price of every loaf","The price of the flour delivery"],"correctAnswer":"The price of the smaller roll","explanation":"The notice promised that the smaller roll would stay the same price.","supportingSentence":"The notice also promised that the smaller roll would stay the same price, so a tight budget still had an option.","skill":"detail"},{"question":"Why was the cheaper item left unchanged?","options":["Someone with little money would still have a choice","The record book could hide the change","Regulars would stop buying bread altogether","The loaf could then be removed from sale"],"correctAnswer":"Someone with little money would still have a choice","explanation":"The roll stayed the same price so a tight budget still had an option.","supportingSentence":"The notice also promised that the smaller roll would stay the same price, so a tight budget still had an option.","skill":"inference"},{"question":"What did the unhappy customer do on Friday?","options":["She stopped buying bread after reading the board","She asked Samir to hide the new price","She took rolls only and left the loaves","She bought her usual loaves and accepted the new price"],"correctAnswer":"She bought her usual loaves and accepted the new price","explanation":"The customer who had grumbled said the bread was worth the new price and bought her usual two loaves.","supportingSentence":"On Friday the customer who had grumbled said the bread was worth the new price and bought her usual two loaves.","skill":"inference"},{"question":"What does grumbled mean in this story?","options":["Wrote a figure in the money records","Paid a higher price gladly","Complained in an unhappy way","Changed from one bread to another"],"correctAnswer":"Complained in an unhappy way","explanation":"Regulars grumbled, and one said she might buy less often, so they were complaining.","supportingSentence":"A few regulars grumbled, and one said she might buy bread less often.","skill":"vocabulary"}],"wordCount":152,"questionCount":5}$q75905$::jsonb,
  '235fe2cd86683d8ed80369ccd2c9cce51e3aa1b71c1eb92c86f3e466ba4dd490',
  true
) on conflict (content_hash) do nothing;

insert into public.exercise_bank (
  id, kind, level, exercise_type, dimensions, payload, content_hash, active
) values (
  '8396c726-c5e6-46f3-adb8-64366b03fb3f'::uuid,
  'reading',
  'B2',
  'text',
  $q4699${"topic":"mystery","wordCount":150,"questionCount":5}$q4699$::jsonb,
  $q49557${"mode":"reading","level":"B2","format":"text","topic":"Mystery","title":"A Card in the Strap","story":"The cafe by the station kept a box of forgotten belongings under the counter. One wet Monday a navy umbrella was still there after closing, with a library card tucked into the strap. The name on the card was worn, but the member number could still be read. Rina could have dropped the umbrella into the box and gone home. Instead she rang the library before it shut. A librarian said the number belonged to a reader who had reported a missing umbrella that afternoon. Rina carried it over on her way to the bus, because the rain was still heavy. At the desk she asked for the number to be checked again before anyone gave the umbrella away. The reader arrived as the lights dimmed, recognised a small tear in the fabric, and thanked them. The card had made a quick return possible while the rain was still falling.","dialogue":[],"vocabulary":[{"word":"forgotten","definition":"Left behind by mistake."},{"word":"strap","definition":"A band that fastens or holds something."},{"word":"librarian","definition":"A person who works in a library."},{"word":"dimmed","definition":"Grew less bright."},{"word":"fabric","definition":"Cloth."},{"word":"recognised","definition":"Knew something from a sign you had seen before."}],"quiz":[{"question":"What was found with the umbrella after closing?","options":["A bus ticket from the morning train","The cafe key under the counter","A torn page from the lost box","A library card tucked into the strap"],"correctAnswer":"A library card tucked into the strap","explanation":"A library card was tucked into the strap of the navy umbrella.","supportingSentence":"One wet Monday a navy umbrella was still there after closing, with a library card tucked into the strap.","skill":"detail"},{"question":"Which part of the card could still be read?","options":["The member number","The colour of the cloth","The cafe address","The owner's full name"],"correctAnswer":"The member number","explanation":"The name was worn, but the member number could still be read.","supportingSentence":"The name on the card was worn, but the member number could still be read.","skill":"detail"},{"question":"What easier choice did Rina turn down?","options":["Closing the cafe before the card was seen","Putting the umbrella in the lost box and going home","Walking through the rain to the library","Asking a reader to point out a tear"],"correctAnswer":"Putting the umbrella in the lost box and going home","explanation":"She could have dropped the umbrella into the box and gone home, but she chose not to.","supportingSentence":"Rina could have dropped the umbrella into the box and gone home.","skill":"inference"},{"question":"What does the ending show about the library card?","options":["It was too worn for any number to be used","It proved that the umbrella belonged to the cafe","It helped get the umbrella back quickly while it was still raining","It stopped the reader from saying thank you"],"correctAnswer":"It helped get the umbrella back quickly while it was still raining","explanation":"The card made a quick return possible while the rain was still falling.","supportingSentence":"The card had made a quick return possible while the rain was still falling.","skill":"inference"},{"question":"What does recognised mean in this story?","options":["Read a number that had worn away","Turned the library lights down","Carried a box of lost belongings","Knew the object from a mark seen before"],"correctAnswer":"Knew the object from a mark seen before","explanation":"The reader recognised a small tear in the fabric, meaning she knew the umbrella by that mark.","supportingSentence":"The reader arrived as the lights dimmed, recognised a small tear in the fabric, and thanked them.","skill":"vocabulary"}],"wordCount":150,"questionCount":5}$q49557$::jsonb,
  '8396c726c5e606f3bdb864366b03fb3f58116f47dc566d1e2b4b008763504b26',
  true
) on conflict (content_hash) do nothing;

insert into public.exercise_bank (
  id, kind, level, exercise_type, dimensions, payload, content_hash, active
) values (
  '4c23c460-90fb-4289-a527-8860d3ae6c79'::uuid,
  'reading',
  'B2',
  'text',
  $q4702${"topic":"mystery","wordCount":153,"questionCount":5}$q4702$::jsonb,
  $q51605${"mode":"reading","level":"B2","format":"text","topic":"Mystery","title":"A Lamp on a Timer","story":"The town archive closed at five, yet the caretaker saw a light in the reading room well after that. He checked the outside first and found every window latch shut. The front door was locked, and the alarm had not sounded. Inside, the light came from a desk lamp, and a page of notes sat under a glass weight. Nobody was among the stacks. He nearly called the police. Then he saw that the plug ran to a small timer on the floor. A researcher had asked, in writing, for the lamp to come on so a damp page would not sit in the dark. The caretaker found that request in the book, signed and dated the same day. The timer had failed to end its cycle, so he switched the lamp off and noted that the device needed replacing. The room had been empty, and the surprising light had a written reason.","dialogue":[],"vocabulary":[{"word":"caretaker","definition":"A person who looks after a building."},{"word":"latch","definition":"A fastening that keeps a window or door shut."},{"word":"stacks","definition":"The shelves where records or books are kept."},{"word":"timer","definition":"A device that switches something at a set time."},{"word":"cycle","definition":"One complete run of a timed action."},{"word":"damp","definition":"Slightly wet."}],"quiz":[{"question":"What did the caretaker find on the outside of the archive?","options":["A researcher climbing through a window","The desk lamp lying in the street","The alarm sounding at the door","Every window fastening was shut"],"correctAnswer":"Every window fastening was shut","explanation":"He checked outside and found every window latch shut.","supportingSentence":"He checked the outside first and found every window latch shut.","skill":"detail"},{"question":"Why had someone asked for the lamp to come on?","options":["A slightly wet page should not be left in the dark","The caretaker wanted to read after closing","The device was meant to fail that night","The police needed light in the shelves"],"correctAnswer":"A slightly wet page should not be left in the dark","explanation":"A researcher asked for the lamp so a damp page would not sit in the dark.","supportingSentence":"A researcher had asked, in writing, for the lamp to come on so a damp page would not sit in the dark.","skill":"detail"},{"question":"What do the locked door and the quiet alarm suggest?","options":["The police were already among the shelves","A crowd had just set the alarm off","Nobody had forced a way into the building","The caretaker had left the front door open"],"correctAnswer":"Nobody had forced a way into the building","explanation":"The front door was locked and the alarm had not sounded, so there was no sign of a break-in.","supportingSentence":"The front door was locked, and the alarm had not sounded.","skill":"inference"},{"question":"What explains the light after closing time?","options":["The room was empty and a written request accounted for the lamp","A stranger was still hiding among the shelves","The lamp had been on since the building opened","The caretaker had invented the request that night"],"correctAnswer":"The room was empty and a written request accounted for the lamp","explanation":"The room had been empty, and the surprising light had a written reason.","supportingSentence":"The room had been empty, and the surprising light had a written reason.","skill":"inference"},{"question":"What does damp mean in this story?","options":["Switched off by a device","Stored on a high shelf","Locked from the outside","Slightly wet"],"correctAnswer":"Slightly wet","explanation":"The lamp was requested for a damp page, meaning a page that was slightly wet.","supportingSentence":"A researcher had asked, in writing, for the lamp to come on so a damp page would not sit in the dark.","skill":"vocabulary"}],"wordCount":153,"questionCount":5}$q51605$::jsonb,
  '4c23c46090fb8289a5278860d3ae6c796ad358727e9504fdfc0578d06ec7e816',
  true
) on conflict (content_hash) do nothing;

insert into public.exercise_bank (
  id, kind, level, exercise_type, dimensions, payload, content_hash, active
) values (
  'f8eb8d21-0e94-4807-a7ae-29911e74c712'::uuid,
  'reading',
  'B2',
  'text',
  $q4879${"topic":"adventure","wordCount":153,"questionCount":5}$q4879$::jsonb,
  $q75920${"mode":"reading","level":"B2","format":"text","topic":"Adventure","title":"One at a Time across the Gap","story":"A winter storm had collapsed part of the coastal path, leaving a narrow gap above the rocks. A warden met the walking group at the gate and explained that the official route now dropped to the beach, but only when the tide was low. Their bus left later that afternoon, and the tide would still be too high for the beach turning. Nia had brought a short rope and offered a handline if the warden judged the rock safe. He tested the holds, set a spare stake on each side, and tied the rope himself. People crossed one at a time, facing the rock, while he watched the line. Packs were passed across separately so nobody leaned out with extra weight. Everyone crossed. They reached the stop muddy and only a little late. Nia coiled the rope and left the stakes with the warden, who planned a proper rail before the next weekend.","dialogue":[],"vocabulary":[{"word":"collapsed","definition":"Fallen down so it could no longer be used."},{"word":"warden","definition":"A person responsible for looking after a place."},{"word":"tide","definition":"The regular rise and fall of the sea."},{"word":"handline","definition":"A fixed rope people hold while they cross."},{"word":"stake","definition":"A pointed piece driven into the ground or rock."},{"word":"coiled","definition":"Wound into neat loops."}],"quiz":[{"question":"When could walkers use the official beach route?","options":["Only at the time of a low sea","Whenever the bus was running late","Only after the cliff path had fallen","At any hour if they carried a rope"],"correctAnswer":"Only at the time of a low sea","explanation":"The official route dropped to the beach only when the tide was low.","supportingSentence":"A warden met the walking group at the gate and explained that the official route now dropped to the beach, but only when the tide was low.","skill":"detail"},{"question":"How did the group cross the gap?","options":["All at once, still wearing their packs","By climbing down with no rope at all","One person at a time, facing the rock, while the line was watched","By waiting on the beach until the bus left"],"correctAnswer":"One person at a time, facing the rock, while the line was watched","explanation":"People crossed one at a time, facing the rock, while the warden watched the line.","supportingSentence":"People crossed one at a time, facing the rock, while he watched the line.","skill":"detail"},{"question":"Why did the beach route not solve the group's problem?","options":["The sea would still be too high before their bus left","Nia refused to walk on sand","The bus stop existed only at noon","The warden had closed the beach for the month"],"correctAnswer":"The sea would still be too high before their bus left","explanation":"Their bus left that afternoon, and the tide would still be too high for the beach turning.","supportingSentence":"Their bus left later that afternoon, and the tide would still be too high for the beach turning.","skill":"inference"},{"question":"Why were the packs passed across on their own?","options":["So the rope could be put away before anyone crossed","So the group would miss the bus on purpose","So the warden could keep the packs","So nobody would lean out while carrying extra weight"],"correctAnswer":"So nobody would lean out while carrying extra weight","explanation":"The packs were passed separately so nobody leaned out with extra weight.","supportingSentence":"Packs were passed across separately so nobody leaned out with extra weight.","skill":"inference"},{"question":"What does collapsed mean in this story?","options":["Fallen away so the path could not be used","Wound into loops for the next weekend","Tested and judged safe to cross","Driven into a crack in the rock"],"correctAnswer":"Fallen away so the path could not be used","explanation":"The storm had collapsed part of the path and left a gap, so that section had fallen away.","supportingSentence":"A winter storm had collapsed part of the coastal path, leaving a narrow gap above the rocks.","skill":"vocabulary"}],"wordCount":153,"questionCount":5}$q75920$::jsonb,
  'f8eb8d210e94d807c7ae29911e74c71200de232df1cec4cd56ad79fc7e83388e',
  true
) on conflict (content_hash) do nothing;

insert into public.exercise_bank (
  id, kind, level, exercise_type, dimensions, payload, content_hash, active
) values (
  '82fc725e-d522-44ac-a0b9-63ac853d101a'::uuid,
  'reading',
  'B2',
  'text',
  $q4663${"topic":"fantasy","wordCount":153,"questionCount":5}$q4663$::jsonb,
  $q83071${"mode":"reading","level":"B2","format":"text","topic":"Fantasy","title":"A Flame for a True Thing","story":"In the hill town of Bramble, a glass lantern woke at dusk only after someone had told it one true thing that day. Lila's lantern stayed dark on the evening of the harvest fair, even though she had talked for hours. She sat on the step and tried again, with an admission that she had pretended to like a song she actually disliked. The lantern flickered and then held a steady flame. Her cousin, waiting in the lane, heard her and laughed instead of taking offence. They took the lantern to the fair. At a stall, a man said his lantern had refused him all week. Lila offered no trick. She said the glass answered honesty, including the uncomfortable kind, and that a boast had never worked for her. He spoke about a mistake at work, and his lantern woke. The fair went on under many flames, each one tied to a truth.","dialogue":[],"vocabulary":[{"word":"dusk","definition":"The time when daylight is fading."},{"word":"admission","definition":"A statement in which you admit the truth."},{"word":"flickered","definition":"Shone in an unsteady way."},{"word":"offence","definition":"Hurt feelings caused by what someone said or did."},{"word":"honesty","definition":"The practice of telling the truth."},{"word":"boast","definition":"Proud talk about yourself."}],"quiz":[{"question":"When does a lantern in this town light?","options":["At the fading of day, after one true statement","Only at the harvest fair, and only for cousins","Whenever a person has talked for several hours","At dawn, after a proud speech"],"correctAnswer":"At the fading of day, after one true statement","explanation":"A lantern woke at dusk only after someone had told it one true thing that day.","supportingSentence":"In the hill town of Bramble, a glass lantern woke at dusk only after someone had told it one true thing that day.","skill":"detail"},{"question":"What truth did Lila tell on the step?","options":["She had refused to attend the harvest fair","She had not spoken to anyone all day","She had broken her cousin's lantern","She had pretended to enjoy a song she did not like"],"correctAnswer":"She had pretended to enjoy a song she did not like","explanation":"Her admission was that she had pretended to like a song she actually disliked.","supportingSentence":"She sat on the step and tried again, with an admission that she had pretended to like a song she actually disliked.","skill":"detail"},{"question":"What does Lila's dark lantern show at the start of the evening?","options":["Her cousin had already taken the flame","The fair had banned every lantern in town","A long conversation was not enough to light it","Proud talk was the only thing the glass accepted"],"correctAnswer":"A long conversation was not enough to light it","explanation":"The lantern stayed dark even though she had talked for hours, so plenty of talk was not enough.","supportingSentence":"Lila's lantern stayed dark on the evening of the harvest fair, even though she had talked for hours.","skill":"inference"},{"question":"What does the cousin's reaction show about their relationship?","options":["He was angry and refused to go any further","He wanted her to speak proudly about the song","The awkward truth did not cause a quarrel between them","He had not heard what she admitted"],"correctAnswer":"The awkward truth did not cause a quarrel between them","explanation":"He heard her and laughed instead of taking offence, so the truth did not start a quarrel.","supportingSentence":"Her cousin, waiting in the lane, heard her and laughed instead of taking offence.","skill":"inference"},{"question":"What does boast mean in this story?","options":["Proud talk, rather than a plain truth","A quiet statement about a mistake","The hour when daylight fades","A small steady flame in glass"],"correctAnswer":"Proud talk, rather than a plain truth","explanation":"Lila says a boast had never worked, unlike honesty, so a boast is proud talk rather than a plain truth.","supportingSentence":"She said the glass answered honesty, including the uncomfortable kind, and that a boast had never worked for her.","skill":"vocabulary"}],"wordCount":153,"questionCount":5}$q83071$::jsonb,
  '82fc725ed52204ac70b963ac853d101aa655a995800e86bf20b8e01cb81efa0f',
  true
) on conflict (content_hash) do nothing;

insert into public.exercise_bank (
  id, kind, level, exercise_type, dimensions, payload, content_hash, active
) values (
  'c4bb88e7-9d38-48c6-acd5-76a9b5664828'::uuid,
